Z||000018||Monkeyman||12-21-1999||05:12 AM||||OK, apparently it's time for me to visit my doctor and get a refill on my prescription.  I didn't notice that Cliffs wreck happened back in March until just now.  Apparently, in addition to my inability to read, write, or walk and chew gum at the same time, I can't tell what the date is either.  Vote for Monkeyman in 1984!!  (Er...I mean 2004)||||reg||9
Z||000019||William Federle||12-21-1999||08:44 AM||||It might be an old thread but it's real interesting to hear how everyone got their fiero. I got mine for $500. (Wisconsin).<BR>It passed emissions the day after I got it.<BR>Since then I've replaced the TPS, the MAP,<BR>water temp gauge sender, O2 sensor, the thermostat, the gasket between the throttle body and the intake manifold, the valve cover gasket and 4 spark plugs. I guess the total cost is getting close to $1000. with the 4 wheels and tires I had to buy after some low life took the originals. I know that I'll have to replace most of the rear ball joints, etc. since the seals on most of them are torn and they won't even hold grease (the ones on the front end all hold grease except for one). I've never had a used car that I<BR>liked so much though. And, it's paid for.<P>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 12-21-1999).]<p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 12-21-1999).]||||reg||10
Z||000020||Warhawk||12-21-1999||02:55 PM||||Howdy all ... I'm new here, and just bought a slick red '86 2m4 coupe for $100. The only catch is that the motor is bad, locked up. I've already got a deal working to install a rebuilt motor at a bargain price by a mechanic who is familiar with Fieros.<P>I'm wondering if there is anything that should be checked or replaced that you wouldn't normally think of? <P>I'm sure I'll need more advice as this project progresses.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000021||ChadMan||12-21-1999||03:31 PM||||The only weak element of the Iron Duke (IMHO) is the nylon timing gear set.  If your rebuilt engine doesn't have new gears, it would be a good idea to have your mechanic install a set while the motor's out of the car.||||reg||1
Z||000022||Wolfhound||12-21-1999||03:42 PM||||Might be a good idea to replace the clutch if it,s a manual transmission while the engines out.||||reg||1
Z||000023||William Federle||12-21-1999||07:24 PM||||It might be a good time to look underneath at<BR>the suspension components and check for torn<BR>seals - ball joints that won't hold grease -<BR>worn components on the front end.||||reg||1
Z||000024||Carrolles||12-22-1999||07:15 AM||||It's a good time to replace the fuel filter and flush out the cooling system.  Take notice of the aluminum heater pipes where they come up the fire wall between the engine and the catalytic converter.  Mine was very corroded where the hose attaches.  It was paper thin.  I cut it back and spliced in a section of hose.  You might need a 3/4" to 5/8" adaptor for this, I did.<P>Also look at the brake hoses and p/brake cable.||||reg||1
Z||000025||TooLowFiero||12-23-1999||10:25 AM||||well i know i dont post much  but i thought i would let ya know of my fieros<BR>84 se paid 100 bucks for (it needed Brakes)<BR>86 2m4 (needed an alignment) bought for 200 bucks both run real good (cept the 86 died yesterday) also on a 86 2m4 for the altenator belt what pulley on the crank does it go on? there are two pulleys one closest to the engine or the one furthest from the engine?  thanks alot <P>Merry Christmas and Happy Hanika (bad speller) =o)||||reg||10
